Recursive insertion works by starting at the root of the tree and
checking whether the current employee is the manager we are looking
for. If the manager is found, the program checks whether the requested
left or right position is available. If it is available, a new
EmployeeNode is created and connected to that position. If the manager
is not found at the current node, the method recursively searches
the left and right subtrees. This continues until the manager is
found or the entire tree has been searched.

One challenge was searching for a manager without accidentally
restarting the search at the root. When a child pointer is None,
the program must stop searching that branch instead of restarting
the insertion process. Another challenge was preventing employees
from being added to positions that are already occupied. The program
handles these situations by checking each pointer before insertion
and displaying helpful error messages when an operation cannot be
completed. It also checks that a valid side is provided.

Trees are useful in real-world systems when information has a
hierarchical structure. Examples include company reporting systems,
computer file directories, website navigation menus, and family
trees. A tree makes relationships between parent and child elements
easy to represent and traverse. Compared with a flat list, a tree
can make it easier to find related information and organize complex
relationships. However, this implementation is a binary tree, so
each employee can have at most two direct reports. A real company
directory may need a general tree that allows managers to supervise
many employees. The current implementation also searches employees
sequentially, taking O(n) time in the worst case for insertion.
